{"title":"JEE Advanced","category":"Engineering","bank":"jee_advanced","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","count":14,"questions":[{"id":26240,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"Two thin wires, Wire-1 of diameter $0.650$ mm and Wire-2 of unknown diameter $d$ are given. To obtain the value of $d$, the diameters of the two wires are measured with a screw gauge. The screw gauge has a pitch of $0.5$ mm and there are $100$ divisions on the circular scale (CS). The smallest division on the linear scale (LS) is $0.5$ mm. The table shows the readings of LS and CS for the measurements. The value of $d$ (in $\\mu$m) is:<br><table style=\"border: 1px solid black; border-collapse: collapse; width: auto;\"><tr><th style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\"></th><th style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\" colspan=\"2\">Readings</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\"></td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">LS (mm)</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">CS</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">Wire-1</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">$0.5$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">$42$</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">Wire-2</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">$1.5$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">$95$</td></tr></table>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"The least count (LC) of the screw gauge is given by:<br><br>$\\text{LC} = \\dfrac{\\text{Pitch}}{\\text{Number of divisions on CS}} = \\dfrac{0.5}{100} = 0.005 \\text{ mm}$<br><br>For Wire-1, the measured reading is:<br><br>$\\text{Reading}_1 = \\text{LS} + \\text{CS} \\times \\text{LC}$<br><br>$\\text{Reading}_1 = 0.5 + 42 \\times 0.005 = 0.5 + 0.210 = 0.710 \\text{ mm}$<br><br>The actual diameter of Wire-1 is $0.650 \\text{ mm}$. The zero error of the screw gauge is:<br><br>$\\text{Zero Error} = \\text{Measured Reading} - \\text{Actual Value} = 0.710 - 0.650 = +0.060 \\text{ mm}$<br><br>For Wire-2, the measured reading is:<br><br>$\\text{Reading}_2 = \\text{LS} + \\text{CS} \\times \\text{LC}$<br><br>$\\text{Reading}_2 = 1.5 + 95 \\times 0.005 = 1.5 + 0.475 = 1.975 \\text{ mm}$<br><br>The actual diameter $d$ of Wire-2 is obtained by subtracting the zero error from its measured reading:<br><br>$d = \\text{Reading}_2 - \\text{Zero Error} = 1.975 - 0.060 = 1.915 \\text{ mm}$<br><br>Converting the diameter into micrometers ($\\mu$m):<br><br>$d = 1.915 \\times 1000  \\mu\\text{m} = 1915  \\mu\\text{m}$<br><br>Answer: $1915$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2026 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"6a0be51444d287c9361e5cc3","questionType":"numerical","_advSection":"NUM","type":"numerical","correctValue":"1915"},{"id":26241,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"Figure 1 shows the configuration of main scale and Vernier scale before measurement. Fig. 2 shows the configuration corresponding to the measurement of diameter $D$ of a tube. The measured value of $D$ is:<br><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FEKbdWkeYJpOThU8dtfa8G8v-55HZQkUnt_F8pHLKPZQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FEKbdWkeYJpOThU8dtfa8G8v-55HZQkUnt_F8pHLKPZQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FEKbdWkeYJpOThU8dtfa8G8v-55HZQkUnt_F8pHLKPZQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","options":["0.12 cm","0.11 cm","0.13 cm","0.14 cm"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned}\n& 10 \\mathrm{MSD}=1 \\mathrm{~cm} ; 1 \\mathrm{MSD}=0.1 \\mathrm{~cm} \\\\\n& 7 \\mathrm{MSD}=10 \\mathrm{VSD} \\\\\n& 1 \\mathrm{VSD}=0.07 \\mathrm{~cm} \\\\\n& \\text { Reading }=2 \\mathrm{MSD}-\\mathrm{VSD} \\\\\n& =0.2 \\mathrm{~cm}-0.07 \\mathrm{~cm}=0.13 \\mathrm{~cm}\n\\end{aligned}$<br>Ans. Option (C)","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2025 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"685102be6bba689a3eb09c82","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26242,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"<p>Area of the cross-section of a wire is measured using a screw gauge. The pitch of the main scale is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. The circular scale has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn></math> divisions and for one full rotation of the circular scale, the main scale shifts by two divisions. The measured readings are listed below.</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 350px;\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>  <td><strong>Measurement condition</strong></td>  <td><strong>Main scale reading</strong></td>  <td><strong>Circular scale reading</strong></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td>Two arms of gauge touching each other without wire</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math> division</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> divisions</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td>Attempt-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>: With wire</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> divisions</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math> divisions</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>  <td>Attempt-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>: With wire</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> divisions</td>  <td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> divisions</td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table><p>What are the diameter and cross-sectional area of the wire measured using the screw gauge?</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mi>mm</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>23</mn><mo> </mo><mo>±</mo><mo> </mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi>mm</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mi>mm</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mi>mm</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<blockquote><p>Note: This question is given bonus by JEE council.</p><p>In one rotation 2 divisions of the main scale are crossed. Therefore, the least count of the screw gauge is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>And the zero error is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>Reading-1</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>MSR</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>LC</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>CSR</mi><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>Zero</mi><mo> </mo><mi>error</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn></mrow></mfenced><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>Reading-2</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn></mrow></mfenced><mi>mm</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>Therefore, average reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>Average mean error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo></math> Diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p><p>Area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>Area</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>Area</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Area</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>±</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>Area</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2022 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b16a9cf23ac7d519f9b5fa","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26243,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"<p>In order to measure the internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> of a cell of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo></math> a meter bridge of wire resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>, a resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, another cell of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> (internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>) and a galvanometer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi></math> are used in a circuit, as shown in the figure. If the null point is found at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>72</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>,</mo></math> then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252F98a0246f-0ceb-4982-9f4e-27c8dce75b43-D9.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2F98a0246f-0ceb-4982-9f4e-27c8dce75b43-D9.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2F98a0246f-0ceb-4982-9f4e-27c8dce75b43-D9.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 155px;\" /> </p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252Fd91e89ea-6c82-4f65-9c6d-67d1f3039373-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Fd91e89ea-6c82-4f65-9c6d-67d1f3039373-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Fd91e89ea-6c82-4f65-9c6d-67d1f3039373-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 241px;\" /></p><p>Resistance of potential wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p><p>Resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> wire <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p><p>So, Resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>72</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> wire <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>50</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>72</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p><p>Current,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>ε</mi></mstyle><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2021 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b16a90f23ac7d519f9b58b","questionType":"numerical","_advSection":"NUM","type":"numerical","correctValue":"3"},{"id":26244,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"<p>The smallest division on the main scale of a Vernier calipers is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. Ten divisions of the Vernier scale correspond to nine divisions of the main scale. The figure below on the left shows the reading of this calipers with no gap between its two jaws. The figure on the right shows the reading with a solid sphere held between the jaws. The correct diameter of the sphere is</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252Ff8c86341-24a8-45c2-a97d-9c345ef2173d-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Ff8c86341-24a8-45c2-a97d-9c345ef2173d-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Ff8c86341-24a8-45c2-a97d-9c345ef2173d-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 179px;\" /></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3.07</mn><mi>cm</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>11</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>17</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi></math></p><p>Least count of the vernier caliper, </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow>