><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>3.15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2021 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b16a88f23ac7d519f9b542","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26245,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"<p>There are two vernier calipers both of which have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> divided into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions on the main scale. The Vernier scale of one of the calipers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions that correspond to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math> main scale divisions. The Vernier scale of the other caliper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions that correspond to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math> main scale divisions. The readings of the two calipers are shown in the figure. The measured values (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>) by calipers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively, are<br ><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FPhy_Papr2_4_A_.jpg%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FPhy_Papr2_4_A_.jpg?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FPhy_Papr2_4_A_.jpg?alt=media\" /><br ><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FPhy_Papr2_4_B_.jpg%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FPhy_Papr2_4_B_.jpg?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FPhy_Papr2_4_B_.jpg?alt=media\" /></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>86</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>82</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>83</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>85</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>82</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>For caliper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>VSD</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi>MSD</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LC</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>MSD</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>VSD</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LC</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>Measured value<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math>Main scale reading<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo></math>vernier scale reading<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>×</mo></math>least count</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>.8</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.01</mo></mrow></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>For caliper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>VSD</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn><mo> </mo><mi>MSD</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LC</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> (Negative least count means we have to take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>VSR</mi></math> from the right of the scale <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>‐</mo><mn>7</mn></math>)</p><p>Measured value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"{\" close=\"}\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>.8</mo><mo>+</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.01</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>83</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2016 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b16a52f23ac7d519f9b35f","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26246,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"Consider a Vernier callipers in which each 1 cm on the main scale is divided into 8 equal divisions and a screw gauge with 100 divisions on its circular scale. In the Vernier callipers, 5 divisions of the Vernier scale coincide with 4 divisions on the main scale and in the screw gauge, one complete rotation of the circular scale moves it by two divisions on the linear scale. Then:","options":["If the pitch of the screw gauge is twice the least count of the Vernier callipers, the least count of the screw gauge is 0.01 mm","If the pitch of the screw gauge is twice the least count of the Vernier callipers, the least count of the screw gauge is 0.005 mm","If the least count of the linear scale of the screw gauge is twice the least count of the Vernier callipers, the least count of the screw gauge is 0.01 mm","If the least count of the linear scale of the screw gauge is twice the least count of the Vernier callipers, the least count of the screw gauge is 0.005 mm"],"answer":1,"solution":"1 main scale division (M.S.D) <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />5 Vernier scale division (V.S.D) <math><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo></mo></math><br  /><math><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math><br  />Least count of Vernier scale <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>.</mo></math><br  /><math><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math><br  /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />For option A and B<br  />If the pitch of the screw gauge is twice the least count of the Vernier callipers then pitch <math><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></math> of Vernier scale<br  /><math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />Hence least count of screw gauge <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></math><br  /><math><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />For option C and D<br  />Least count of linear scale of screw gauge<br  /><math><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />Pitch <math><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />Least count of screw gauge <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br  />Hence answer is (B, C)","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2015 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b16a40f23ac7d519f9b2b6","_advSection":"MC","questionType":"multipleCorrect","type":"multipleCorrect","answers":[1,2]},{"id":26247,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"<p>During an experiment with a metre bridge, the galvanometer shows a null point when the jockey is pressed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm </mi></math>using a standard resistance of <math><mn>90</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> ,as shown in the figure. The least count of the scale used in the metre bridge is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>mm</mi></math>. The unknown resistance is<br ><br/><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252Fimage9-b3319f15-d980-4e53-940f-9365525b918d.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Fimage9-b3319f15-d980-4e53-940f-9365525b918d.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2Fimage9-b3319f15-d980-4e53-940f-9365525b918d.png?alt=media\" /></p>","options":["<math><mn>60</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>","<math><mn>135</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.56</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>","<math><mn>60</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>","<math><mn>135</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.23</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>The unknown resistance is given by, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mn>90</mn></math><br  />Putting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p><p>For error analysis, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mn>90</mn></math><br  />In multiplication and division, fractional errors get added up.</p><p>Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mn>60</mn></mstyle></mfrac></math><br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mn>40</mn></mfenced><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mn>60</mn></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>60</mn></math><math><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math><br  />Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2014 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b16a39f23ac7d519f9b279","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26248,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"During Searle's experiment, zero of the Vernier scale lies between <math><mn>3.20</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math><mn>3.25</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> of the main scale. The <math><msup><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></msup></math> division of the Vernier scale exactly coincides with one of the main scale divisions. When an additional load of 2 kg is applied to the wire, the zero of the Vernier scale still lies between <math><mn>3.20</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math><mn>3.25</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> of the main scale but now the <math><msup><mrow><mn>45</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></msup></math> division of Vernier scale coincides with one of the main scale divisions. The length of the thin metallic wire is 2 m and its cross-sectional area is <math><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> . The least count of the Vernier scale is <math><mn>1.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> . The maximum percentage error in the Young's modulus of the wire is","options":[],"answer":"4","solution":"<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>  since the experiment measures only change in the length of wire<br > <math><mo>∴</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn></math> <br >From the observation  <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> <br > <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>45</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> <br > <math><mo>⇒</mo></math>  change in lengths  <math><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> <br >and the maximum permissible error in elongation is one LC<br > <math><mo>∴</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>25</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2014 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b16a33f23ac7d519f9b240","_advSection":"NUM","questionType":"numerical","type":"numerical","correctValue":"4"},{"id":26249,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":" The diameter of a cylinder is measured using a vernier calipers with no zero error. It is found that the zero of the vernier scale lies between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> of the main scale. The vernier scale has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn></math>  divisions equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>24</mn><mi>th</mi></msup></math> division of the vernier scale exactly coincides with one of the main scale divisions. The diameter of the cylinder is, ","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>112</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>124</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>136</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi><mo> </mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>148</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<math> <mrow> <mtext>1 MSD</mtext> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>0.05 ​</mtext></mrow></math><br  /><math> <mrow> <mtext>1 VSD</mtext> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mtext>2.45</mtext> </mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn> <mn>0</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>0.04</mtext> </mrow> </math><br  /><math> <mrow> <mtext>Diameter of the cylinder</mtext> </mrow> </math> <br  /><math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>MSR</mtext> <mo>+</mo> <mtext>L.C.</mtext> <mo>×</mo> <mn>2</mn> <mn>4</mn> </mrow> </math><br  /><math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>5.124 cm</mtext> </mrow> </math>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2013 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b16a25f23ac7d519f9b1c2","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26250,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"A vernier calipers has $1 \\mathrm{~mm}$ marks on the main scale. It has 20 equal divisions on the vernier scale which match with 16 main scale divisions. For this vernier calipers, the least count is","options":["$0.02 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.05 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.1 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.2 \\mathrm{~mm}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Least count of vernier calipers\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\text { LC }=1 \\mathrm{MSD}-1 \\text { VSD } \\\\\n& =\\frac{\\text { Smallest division on main scale }}{\\text { Number of divisions on vernier scale }} \\\\\n& 20 \\text { divisions of vernier scale } \\\\\n& =16 \\text { divisions of main scale } \\\\\n& \\therefore \\quad 1 \\mathrm{VSD}=\\frac{16}{20} \\mathrm{~mm}=0.8 \\mathrm{~mm} \\\\\n& \\therefore \\quad \\text { LC }=1 \\mathrm{MSD}-1 \\mathrm{VSD} \\\\\n& =1 \\mathrm{~mm}-0.8 \\mathrm{~mm} \\\\\n& \\therefore \\quad=0.2 \\mathrm{~mm} \\\\\n&\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$\\therefore$ The correct option is (d).","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2010 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b16a04f23ac7d519f9b090","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26251,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"Column I gives a list of possible set of parameters measured in some experiments. The variations of the parameters in the form of graphs are shown in Column II. Match the set of parameters given in Column I with the graphs given in Column II. Indicate your answer by darkening the appropriate bubble of the $4 \\times 4$ matrix given in the ORS.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FkKOnEC0VsHIYxeGj56aR3d6HdmJGw3KZd3eJoq8bRzU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FkKOnEC0VsHIYxeGj56aR3d6HdmJGw3KZd3eJoq8bRzU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FkKOnEC0VsHIYxeGj56aR3d6HdmJGw3KZd3eJoq8bRzU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","options":["(A) q, (B) p,q, (C) p,s, (D) q,s","(A) q,r, (B) p,q, (C) s, (D) q","(A) p, (B) q,s, (C) s, (D) q","(A) p,q, (B) q, (C) p,q, (D) q"],"answer":2,"solution":"(A) Potential energy is minimum at mean position.\n(B) For $a=0, s=v t \\longrightarrow$ option $(q)$\nor $\\quad s=s_0+v t \\longrightarrow$ option $(r)$\nFor $a=$ constant,\n\ns=u t+\\frac{1}{2} a t^2 \\longrightarrow \\text { option }(s)\n\n(C) $R=\\frac{v^2 \\sin 2 \\theta}{g} \\therefore R \\propto v^2 \\longrightarrow$ option (s)\n(D) $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}} \\therefore T^2 \\propto l \\longrightarrow$ option $(q)$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2008 (Paper 2)","_marksId":"64b169e9f23ac7d519f9af96","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26252,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"Students I, II and III perform an experiment for measuring the acceleration due to gravity $(g)$ using a simple pendulum. They use different lengths of the pendulum and/or record time for different number of oscillations. The observations are shown in the table.\nLeast count for length $=0.1 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nLeast count for time $=0.1 \\mathrm{~s}$\n\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FvlvGSLty-Nbgt7ypLoTmhc8v_JV4M1IqFcYxvSHpk0I.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FvlvGSLty-Nbgt7ypLoTmhc8v_JV4M1IqFcYxvSHpk0I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FvlvGSLty-Nbgt7ypLoTmhc8v_JV4M1IqFcYxvSHpk0I.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n\nIf $E_{\\mathrm{I}}, E_{\\mathrm{II}}$ and $E_{\\mathrm{III}}$ are the percentage errors in $g$, i.e., $\\left(\\frac{\\Delta g}{g} \\times 100\\right)$ for students I, II and III, respectively.","options":["$E_{\\mathrm{I}}=0$","$E_{\\mathrm{I}}$ is minimum","$E_{\\mathrm{I}}=E_{\\mathrm{II}}$","$E_{\\mathrm{II}}$ is maximum"],"answer":1,"solution":"\n\\begin{aligned}\n& T & =2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}} \\quad \\text { or } \\quad \\frac{t}{n}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}} \\\\\n\\therefore \\quad & g & =\\frac{\\left(4 \\pi^2\\right)\\left(n^2\\right) l}{t^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$\\%$ error in $g=\\frac{\\Delta g}{g} \\times 100=\\left(\\frac{\\Delta l}{l}+\\frac{2 \\cdot \\Delta t}{t}\\right) \\times 100$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& E_{\\mathrm{I}}=\\left(\\frac{0.1}{64}+\\frac{2 \\times 0.1}{128}\\right) \\times 100=0.3125 \\% \\\\\n& E_{\\mathrm{II}}=\\left(\\frac{0.1}{64}+\\frac{2 \\times 0.1}{64}\\right) \\times 100=0.46875 \\% \\quad E_{\\mathrm{III}}=\\left(\\frac{0.1}{20}+\\frac{2 \\times 0.1}{36}\\right) \\times 100=1.055 \\%\n\\end{aligned}\n\nHence, $E_{\\mathrm{I}}$ is minimum.\n$\\therefore$ correct option is (b).","difficulty":"Medium","source":"JEE Advanced 2008 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b169dff23ac7d519f9af40","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":26253,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"JEE Advanced","q":"The circular scale of a screw gauge has 50 divisions and pitch of $0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}$. Find the diameter of sphere. Main scale reading is 2 .\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_advanced%252FVASXFazDcy_MWn37WFedHJCS7GriIFZHYPqEImRmNPU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FVASXFazDcy_MWn37WFedHJCS7GriIFZHYPqEImRmNPU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_advanced%2FVASXFazDcy_MWn37WFedHJCS7GriIFZHYPqEImRmNPU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","options":["$1.2$","$1.25$","$2.20$","$2.25$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Least count $\\mathrm{LC}=\\frac{\\text { Pitch }}{\\text { Number of divisions on circular scale }}=\\frac{0.1}{50}=0.01 \\mathrm{~mm}$\nNow, diameter of ball $=(2 \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~mm})+(25-5)(0.01)=1.2 \\mathrm{~mm}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2006","_marksId":"64b169c5f23ac7d519f9ae4b","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"}]}